\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens On Government  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Inflation Bets\b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2011\par 15:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real opened weaker on Monday as investors see government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market holding down potential gains and as inflation bets stabilize, signaling less need for significantly higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at 1.5795 to the dollar, according to Telekurs via FactSet. That's weaker than Friday's close of BRL1.5745.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect Brazilian inflation to end this year at 6.31%, and next year at 5.2%, according to a weekly survey conducted by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Both estimates are unchanged from the previous survey as economists begin to see the effect of already high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Economists also maintained expectations that the benchmark Selic rate will rise to 12.75% at the end of this year, from 12.25% currently. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets this week and investors are expecting a quarter-point rate increase.\par \par \uc2 One of the highest interest rates in the world has attracted massive investment to Latin America's biggest economy, pushing up the value of the real. The looming rate, together with a hot domestic consumer market and high prices for the country's mineral and agricultural commodity exports, has fed into demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Foreign investment in the country has picked up this month, with daily inflows reaching an average of $254 million in July, according to Banco Santander.\par \par \uc2 But a steady stream of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from higher taxes to rising bank reserve requirements, has mitigated gains. The latest move was to restrict banks' net short position on the U.S. dollar, announced July 8.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to see the BRL testing the 1.55 record low, but we now believe that the potential beyond that is more modest on the back of more elevated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks," Banco Santander economist Alejandro Estevez-Breton wrote in a note Monday.\par \par \uc2 The rate broke past 1.55 earlier this month, surpassing a 2008 record to trade at the strongest level since the country instituted a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999.\par \par \uc2 -By Paulo Winterstein, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7073; paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com [ 18-07-11 1302GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110718e77i000ue\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
