\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Market Strengthens, Casts Wary Eye Abroad\b0\par\par\uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2011\par 21:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real strengthens, but Europe's debt troubles remain on the radar\par \par \uc2 --U.S. dollar loses ground against global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Federal Reserve releases minutes from June meeting\par \par \uc2 --Federal Reserve discusses exit from easy-money policies, but end unlikely in near future\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real exits active trading at BRL1.5795 to the dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Jeff Fick \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market strengthened Tuesday, rebounding from early session losses related to debt troubles abroad amid signs that the end to easy-money policies in the U.S. may not be near.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trading session kicked off with renewed concerns about the fiscal health of Europe, where several of the European Union's lesser members are struggling with heavy debt loads and sluggish growth. The latest victim is Italy, which was forced to pay higher-than-expected interest rates in a successful bond sale. Yields on Spanish bonds also soared due to fears of contagion after Greece last month narrowly avoided the single-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  zone's first debt default.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market in general remained tense because of the situation in Europe," said Alberto Orsovay, a trader at Sao Paulo-based brokerage house Hencorp Commcor. "It looks like the hole is getting deeper and it's going to be awhile before Europe's troubles are resolved."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European debt troubles undermined the euro, which fell to an all-time low against the Swiss franc and its lowest level in four months against the greenback. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tumbled further after Moody's Investors Service downgraded the sovereign debt of Ireland to Ba1, with a negative outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  received a boost late in the session, when the U.S. Federal Reserve released minutes from its June meeting. Fed officials took a wait-and-see approach at the meeting as the world's largest economy continued to struggle. The Fed discussed how to unwind its asset-purchase program designed to stimulate the economy, but the minutes emphasized that the discussion "did not imply that a move toward such normalization would necessarily begin sometime soon."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed minutes "sent the dollar a little lower," noted Hencorp Commcor's Orsovay.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian real shrugged off the latest market uncertainty to exit active trading at BRL1.5795, according to Tullet Prebon via Factset. That was slightly stronger from Monday's closing quote at BRL1.5801 to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real's surprising strength came despite last Friday's measures to crimp bank's short-dollar positions, with banks having five days to comply. Brazilian banks will now have to deposit 60% of the value of their short-dollar positions above $1 billion without remuneration, which will boost local demand for dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The measure had an immediate impact on the cost of borrowing dollars in Brazil, known as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  coupon, with interest rates on dollar loans jumping. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  move is aimed at stemming so-called "hot money" short-term inflows into Brazil and at curtailing the real's recent strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the market's quick adjustment to the move, volatility could be heightened this week "as some banks are likely to unwind their net short-dollar positions via buying foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot and selling foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the futures and forwards curves," said Banco Santander.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also indicates that central bankers have drawn a "line in the sand" at BRL1.55 to the dollar, BofA \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  implemented the latest reserve requirement and held its first reverse-swap auction since March on Friday, far from a "coincidence" after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had broken through a key technical barrier at BRL1.5519 to the dollar--the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strongest level since August 2008, the firm said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures can be expected if the real keeps getting stronger, with moves likely to be unpredictable because "there is a big toolbox that could be used," BofA \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But government officials face a difficult task in reining in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with sky-high local interest rates attracting heavy foreign investor interest. Brazil's benchmark Selic base interest rate stands at 12.25%, one of the highest benchmark rates in the world. Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to raise the Selic once again later this month in an effort to tamp down domestic demand and contain rising prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the market has already priced in [an interest rate rise], but it could increase inflows," said Hencorp Commcor's Orsovay. For this week, however, the real is likely to remain rangebound between BRL1.57 and BRL1.60 to the dollar unless there is a resolution or further bad news out of Europe, the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 jeff.fick@dowjones.com [ 12-07-11 1950GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110712e77c0019g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
