\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Weaker On Europe Woes, US Jobs Data\b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2011\par 21:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  government steps up market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Global risk aversion could persist next week\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real seen weaker next week on Europe woes\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Murphy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real was weaker against the U.S. dollar at the end of regular trading Friday as global risk aversion rose in the face of renewed European debt worries and disappointing U.S. jobs data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real finished active trading at BRL1.5627 to the dollar, weaker against Thursday's close of BRL1.5556, according to Tullet Prebon via Factset.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro dropped against the dollar Friday on reports that Italian Finance Minister Giulio Tremonti might resign over disagreements on how to handle the country's debt problems. The reports come at an especially bad time for Europe, with Greece already teetering on the brink of default and severe debt problems plaguing Spain, Ireland and Portugal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the U.S. reported creation of only 18,000 jobs in June. Analysts had expected more than 100,000 new jobs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One Brazilian traders said, "At first, we thought they had left off a zero when releasing the figure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The combination of European debt woes and a sluggish U.S. economic recovery led to rising risk aversion among investors everywhere.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Risk aversion is likely to continue next week," said Marcos Morais, a trader at Sao Paulo's Intercam brokerage. "We're forecasting some slight additional weakening for the real against the dollar."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday stepped up its market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in an apparent attempt to "take advantage" of risk aversion to roll back some of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent gains, said Morais.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazilian officials have been keen to stall, or even reverse, the real's appreciation against the dollar. The strong real hurts exporters and manufacturers. The real has gained 20% against the dollar over the past two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market three times Friday, twice with snap auctions to buy dollars and once with a reverse swap auction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reverse swap auctions offer investors the opportunity to exchange dollar-linked bonds for paper indexed to domestic interest rates. The auctions tend to support the dollar by removing future dollar inflows that overhang the market. At Friday's auction, the government sold reverse swaps equal to $483 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lower international commodities prices Friday also contributed to a weakening of the real. Lower commodities prices mean reduced export revenues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On credit markets Friday, investors showed renewed worries about inflation pressure and possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  interest-rate hikes to come. Analysts at RBC bank in a research note forecast a quarter-point rate hike in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's base rate in July and another quarter-point hike in August. The base rate is already a towering 12.25%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, interest rate futures contracts closed mostly higher Friday. The contracts reflect investor expectations for annualized interest rates at future dates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among actively traded contracts, that of January 2012 closed at 12.50%, up from 12.49% Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 08-07-11 1951GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110708e7780017c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
