\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slide on weak US jobs data\b0\par\par\uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2011\par 19:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * U.S. jobs data worst in 9 months; hits growth outlook\par \par \uc2 * Mexico, Chile pesos, Brazil real weaken vs dollar\par \par \uc2 * Brazil buys dollars, sells swaps to check real's gains\par \par \uc2 * Foreign bets on stronger real reach record Thursday\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds second Brazil dollar auction)\par \par \uc2 By Jeb Blount and Jean Luis Arce\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO/MEXICO CITY (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the dollar Friday, led by Mexico's peso and Brazil's real, as weak U.S. job creation dimmed expectations for growth in the world's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Job growth in the United States almost came to a halt in June with nonfarm employers hiring 18,000 workers, the weakest reading since September. The unemployment rate rose to 9.2 percent, a six-month high, the U.S. Labor Department said.\par \par \uc2 The data comes after a separate report Thursday signaled robust private-sector job growth and helped Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surge to or near to record highs.\par \par \uc2 "These numbers were clearly disappointing and break the momentum of gains in recent weeks," said Alejandro Padilla, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banorte-IXE in Mexico City. "Yesterday's gains have helped increase the size of today's falls."\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso weakened 0.7 percent to 11.6202 to the U.S. dollar, putting the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on track for its biggest one-day slide since June 16 and its first weekly loss -- 0.2 percent -- in a month.\par \par \uc2 The peso's declines were the largest of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mexico's close relationship with its larger northern neighbor -- it gets 80 percent of its export earnings from the United States -- makes it more dependent on U.S. trends than other major Latin American economies.\par \par \uc2 "This was a very bad day for the world economy and for the prospects of global recovery because the data shows that the United States is suffering in its recovery and that it is very weak," said Benito Berber, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Nomura Securities in New York. "This obviously has clear consequences for Mexican growth expectations."\par \par \uc2 The slowing U.S. economy makes it likely that the peso will slip to the 11.70 to 11.80 range, he added.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real also weakened, shedding 0.5 percent to 1.563 to the dollar. The declines come as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets. The real shed 0.2 percent this week.\par \par \uc2 "I've been concerned about U.S. growth for some time, but the jobs number today was even worse than most expected," said Carlos Kawall, chief economist with Banco Safra SA in Sao Paulo. "This does not bode well for world growth."\par \par \uc2 Despite the real's decline, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to help the real weaken even further.\par \par \uc2 During morning trading Friday, the bank bought dollars and sold reverse-swap contracts at auction to drive up demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while putting pressure on the real to weaken. It held a second dollar auction in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The auctions come days after comments from Finance Minister Guido Mantega suggesting the government might act to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 7 percent gain this year. Mantega's comments, though, have gone largely unheeded by foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Friday's declines in the real came a day after foreign bets the real will gain further reached a record high Wednesday on Sao Paulo's BM&F exchange.\par \par \uc2 Foreign "net real longs" rose 5.6 percent to a notional value of $24.6 billion on Thursday -- the most recent day for which data is available -- from $23.3 billion on Wednesday, according to the BM&F and Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The net real longs represent bets that the real will gain minus the bets the real will weaken made by nonresident individuals and companies using two BM&F contracts -- the dollar future and cupom cambial DDI future.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso slipped 0.3 percent to 462.45 to the dollar, easing back from its strongest in three months. It closed the week 0.6 percent stronger than last Friday.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso was little changed, gaining 45 centavos to 1,760.45 to the dollar and extending Thursday's eight-year high. Gains for the week were 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The day after Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held the country's benchmark lending rate at 4.25 percent, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, was little changed at 2.7420 for a weekly gain of 0.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Jeb Blount; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110711e778001a5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
