\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  More If  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Crosses 2008 Peaks\b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 22:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Some more gains seen possible for the Brazilian real\par \par \uc2 -BRL1.55 per dollar seen as a key level for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Anjali Cordeiro \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real has rallied for two weeks and investment flows may push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a little further, although policymakers are expected to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more forcefully if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthens past the highs it reached in 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real was recently at BRL1.5755 per dollar, according to CQG, its strongest level since its August 2008 peak of about BRL1.56 per dollar. The previous peak was reached just before the global economic crisis exploded.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Enrique Alvarez, an analyst at IDEAGlobal says that after the recent lengthy rally the market will try and test the 2008 peak and BRL1.55 is the next key level to watch.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That's where I think the market wants to trend and test. That's where the authorities will feel obliged to take another stand," he said. Allowing an unfettered appreciation of the real would cause problems for domestic exporters as well as create an overflow of Chinese imports, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real has strengthened past several key levels in recent weeks, although Brazilian authorities have tried to stem the appreciation by building dollar reserves and by extending an existing 6% tax on short-term foreign loans to loans of up to two years. Still, those measures weren't viewed as very stringent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the real has strengthened investors have grown more hopeful that Brazil, which has aggressively fought \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation in the past, is becoming more willing to permit a stronger real. Brazil, like much of the emerging world, faces rising inflation and a stronger real would help the country fight inflationary pressures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government figures released Thursday showed a rise in the 12-month inflation rate to 6.3% as of March from 6.01% as of February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS strategist Flavia Cattan-Naslausky feels that it is "very doable" for the real to appreciate to BRL1.55 per dollar. The risk of the government introducing draconian capital controls has abated, she said. "The government doesn't want to risk any disorderly selloff in the real triggered by draconian measures. Now the big game changer is inflation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's government on Thursday adopted a new 1.5% financial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tax on virtually all forms of consumer credit in a bid to help reduce galloping inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policymakers could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciates past further key levels, Cattan-Naslausky said. But she expects such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to remain fairly orthodox and to be done through measures such as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  building dollar reserves or a fine-tuning of existing taxes and measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said Brazil will enact future measures to contain its strengthening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to avoid asset bubbles that could harm its economy should the steady inflow of foreign investment reverse.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since March 28, the dollar has declined about 5% against the real. Still, investors aren't sure just how much Brazilian authorities will be able to do over the long term to control appreciation and heavy investment flows. At a towering 11.75%, Brazil's benchmark interest rate is among the world's highest, making the country an attractive investment destination.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As much as they want to limit the appreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , there is certainly a very large need for foreign financing...foreign direct investment," said Roberto Sanchez-Dahl, an emerging-market portfolio manager with Federated Investors, who manages about $1.1 billion in assets out of Pittsburgh.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreigners aren't the only ones to blame for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciating as local investors are also making speculative bets on the real, he said. His team has positions in real-denominated debt in Brazil, but they aren't making too many changes to their positions because taxes announced last year on foreign fixed income investments make it costlier to buy and sell such assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anjali Cordeiro, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200; anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Paulo Winterstein and Tom Murphy contributed to this article. [ 08-04-11 2034GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110408e748001a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
