\par\par \b \uc2 Traders Eye  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Real Starts Week Quietly\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Cowley   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2011\par 14:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--After last week's tumultous trading, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market began the week on a quiet note, and with little in terms of data to drive the market on Monday, all eyes will be on government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real gained nearly 3.4% last week against the dollar, as money flooded into Brazil and pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  right past the BRL1.65 level which had been seen as an unofficial barrier. Banks piled on the pressure in what traders attempted as a battle of wills with the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the real opened slightly weaker, trading at BRL1.6190 according to Tullett Prebon via Factset, slightly weaker from the level BRL1.6156 at which it exited active trading on Friday.\par \par \uc2 As long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is steady, it seems unlikely the government will come out with any forceful capital control measures, although there is plenty of speculation, said Alberto Orsovay, of the Hencorp Commcor brokerage. In the meantime, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to turn to its regular auctions to buy dollars, or offer reverse swap contracts, he said.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves increased $9.6 billion in March from the previous month on continued dollar purchases in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to figures published on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's website Monday.\par \par \uc2 Foreign reserves totaled $317.1 billion as of March 31, up from $307.5 billion at the end of February.\par \par \uc2 In the first three months of this year, reserves grew by $28.6 billion. Reserves began growing sharply when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started purchasing dollars from the spot market in October 2005. They totaled $53.779 billion at the end of 2005.\par \par \uc2 They declined beginning in October 2008 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loaned dollars to Brazilian businesses in the face of the global credit crunch. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold dollars from the reserves to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market via spot auctions.\par \par \uc2 In mid-2009, however, the government reversed course, buying dollars from the market at spot auctions since then.\par \par \uc2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11 3544 7082; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com [ 04-04-11 1237GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110404e744000v2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
