\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Oil Prices 'Sideways' Since UN No-Fly Zone Began\b0\par\par\uc2 1,249 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:51 (Dow Jones) Oil prices have moved "sideways" since the UN authorized a no-fly zone in Libya earlier this month, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  oil analysts point out. "At some point on each of the past 11 trading days, front-month Brent futures have reached $115.50/bbl," they write. "Perhaps surprisingly, the involvement of external forces in Libya under the UN resolution has so far been associated with a reduction in volatility." But they add that the $115/bbl equilibrium may be unstable. A sudden decline in geopolitical tensions would send Brent to $90, they say. Brent crude up $1.64 at $116.77. Nymex crude up $1.59 at $105.86. (dan.strumpf@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:48 (Dow Jones) Fourth-quarter net profits at Brazilian meatpacker \uc2 Marfrig Alimentos\uc2  (MRFG3.BR), the world's fourth-largest, show the company is making progress as it integrates recently acquired rivals Seara and \uc2 Keystone Foods\uc2 , says Link Investimentos. Better capacity utilization in Brazil and higher exports to Europe offset increased prices for livestock and feed, firm says. "The fourth-quarter numbers present an improvement from the previous quarter, and the market should react positively," firm says. Shares up 0.5% at BRL14.10 in Sao Paulo. (jeff.fick@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:46 (Dow Jones) Rated defaults of municipal bonds are likely to rise in 2011, reaching two to three times the number seen in 2008, says Bob Kurtter, managing director for US public finance at \uc2 Moody's\uc2 . That would mean 12 to 15 rated defaults. "All of this depends on what we're seeing state and local government officials doing across the country, which is making the tough decisions that need to be made to address their problems," Kurtter says at a Federal Reserve conference in Charlotte. (jeffrey.sparshott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 (Dow Jones) The British press is buzzing about builder \uc2 Taylor Wimpey\uc2  (TW.LN) selling its North American \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which include a large private builder that has \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Arizona and Texas - for about $955M to investors including TPG Capital. But on this side of the pond, the deal isn't generating much chatter because nothing really changes. "It doesn't appear to change the competitive landscape at all," says competitor \uc2 Meritage Homes\uc2 ' (MTH) \uc2 Brent Anderson\uc2 . (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:40 (Dow Jones) Grain traders fear season-end US corn supplies could reach a record low near 500M bushels unless high prices slow demand. The US next week is expected to cut its current forecast of 675M, a 15-year low, after reporting Thursday that March 1 inventories were tighter than expected. \uc2 MaxYield Cooperative\uc2  in Iowa reports talk of season-end supplies dropping to 490M, while an exchange-traded fund manager says he hears forecasts for 500M. "That is terrifyingly low," says Sal Gilbertie, lead portfolio manager of The \uc2 Teucrium Corn Fund\uc2 . CBOT May corn futures remain up the daily 30c limit at $6.93 1/4 a bushel. (tom.polansek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:37 (Dow Jones) Bond prices stay marginally lower. Investors are digesting the release, which says the banks there face EUR24B capital shortfall. The Irish government has tapped EU bailout fund to rescue the struggling banking system. The benchmark US 10-year note is 1/32 lower, yielding 3.45%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:36 (Dow Jones) Pratt & Whitney now two-for-two over GE in the battle to provide new engines for the Airbus A320neo. The big order from IndiGo for the United Technologies (UTX) unit will see the contest move on to \uc2 Deutsche Lufthansa\uc2  (DLA.XE), an industry bellwether whose choice could give the Pratt GTF engine even more momentum, or allow GE's Leap-X joint venture to regain some ground. ILFC, the leasing company, has already chosen the GTF for 60 of the 100 A320neos it has on order. \uc2 Virgin America\uc2  has also yet to decide on its engine choice. (doug.cameron@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:33 (Dow Jones) Right before it failed in 2008, \uc2 Washington Mutual\uc2  was a heavy Fed borrower at the discount window, new data reveal, confirming at least the historical norm that desperate borrowers go there at last resort. The first time it appears that September in 2008 was Thursday, Sept. 18, a week before it went down, taking $2B to carry the weekend. Starting Monday, when that first loan was due, it turned to taking $2B overnight each night. It even took $2B on Thursday. Sept. 25, as \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (JPM) took it over late that day in the biggest bank failure in history. Last resort wasn't enough for \uc2 WaMu\uc2 . (david.benoit@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:14 (Dow Jones) Middleburg Financial (MBRG) shares on a tear today as investors digest David Sokol's comments that he'd be interested in starting his own investment company modeled on \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  (BRKA, BRKB). Sokol, who said he did nothing wrong in buying shares of \uc2 Lubrizol\uc2  (LZ) before Berkshire's purchase, is the largest shareholder in Middleburg with about a 20% stake. Sandler O'Neill analyst Casey Orr says shares up 9.3% to $16.45 on nearly twice average daily volume as traders "speculating what's going to happen next." MBRG wasn't immediately available for comment. (corrie.driebusch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:09 (Dow Jones) Stockpiles of natural gas in traditional gas-producing states hit the highest level ever recorded in March in the EIA's weekly storage report, a bearish data point amid the widely held view that supply will overwhelm demand during the first half of this year. Gas in storage in the region rises to 740B cubic feet last week, 25% above 2010 levels. Last year, when a similar supply imbalance pressured the market, inventories didn't reach that level until the end of April. Nymex May gas recently down 2.2% at $4.259/MMBtu. EIA has tracked regional inventories since 1994. (matt.day@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:04 (Dow Jones) The USDA's lower-than-expected estimate for corn inventories as of March 1 fuels fears that season-end supplies will drop to a record low. Government could slash 50M-75M bushels from the latest season-end supply estimate of 675M in a crop report next week, says Rich Nelson at Allendale. Season-end supplies are already at a 15-year low, representing 18 days worth of corn, he notes. CBOT May corn trades up the daily 30c limit to $6.93 1/4 a bushel. (tom.polansek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:03 (Dow Jones) After tepid, routine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  turns aggressive in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. In morning trading, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars at a snap auction and then calls an auction of reverse swap contracts that could sweep up as much as $1.5B in future dollar deliveries. More \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could follow in the afternoon. The bank is sweating to arrest further unwanted appreciation of the Brazilian real, which gained sharply against the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. In late morning trading, the real is again stronger against the dollar at BRL1.6244 versus BRL1.6275 at the close Wednesday. (tom.murphy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit 