\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Aggressive In  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 1,173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:03 (Dow Jones) After tepid, routine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  turns aggressive in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. In morning trading, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars at a snap auction and then calls an auction of reverse swap contracts that could sweep up as much as $1.5B in future dollar deliveries. More \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could follow in the afternoon. The bank is sweating to arrest further unwanted appreciation of the Brazilian real, which gained sharply against the American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. In late morning trading, the real is again stronger against the dollar at BRL1.6244 versus BRL1.6275 at the close Wednesday. (tom.murphy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:01 (Dow Jones) While \uc2 Entergy\uc2  (ETR) says it completed negotiations on a 20-year power-purchase agreement with Vermont's third-largest utility, the Vermont Electric Cooperative's board is "struggling" and divided about approving the contract, VEC CEO David Hallquist says in an interview. "\uc2 Entergy\uc2  has a very bad track record in Vermont" about leaks of toxic materials and other safety issues, and those concerns have risen with the Japan nuclear crisis, he notes. VEC's 12-member board opts to take another month to gauge constituent sentiment before voting. If the contract is dropped, that could strengthen state lawmakers' hand in shutting down the Vermont Yankee plant. Federal regulators have said it is safe extend \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 2022. ETR down 1% at $67.34. (naureen.malik@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:00 (Dow Jones) US inflation will rise enough this year to make Federal Reserve officials "squirm," \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  Chief Economist Mickey Levy says. "I think core inflation will be above 1.5% by mid-year and 2% by year end. I think the big concern here is a spike in inflationary expectations," Levy says at a Fed conference in Charlotte. (jeffrey.sparshott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:57 (Dow Jones) Brazil's government appears to be hearing the calls from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for greater restraint on spending, says Gradual Investimetos. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it clear that reining in spending is "sine qua non condition for a less aggressive monetary policy," it says. Government accounts published Thursday show the primary surplus is edging closer to its target for year end. "From what we can see, the homework is being done." (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:48 (Dow Jones) Some block trades occur in bond futures and the selling spills over into the cash market. Thin trading exaggerates the move as many investors await Friday's payrolls data. The benchmark 10-year note is now 7/32 lower, yield 3.470%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  was a major Treasurys bull until late last year. Now the top bond dealer says the growth/inflation mix has become less "bond friendly" of late and its rate strategists see higher 5-year yields, betting they will rise to 2.5% in coming months, compared to the 2.3% previously called. Correspondingly, the benchmark 10-year yield, recently at 3.45%, should trade in a 3.50-3.75% range in 2Q, they say in their monthly bond-market research report. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:32 (Dow Jones) Jeff Matthews, the founder of hedge fund Ram Partners and author of "Pilgrimage to \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 's Omaha" isn't a Buffett apologist, but he isn't often a harsh critic of the company either. Consider that as you digest his take on David Sokol's purchase of \uc2 Lubrizol\uc2  (LZ) shares before recommending Buffett buy the company outright. "If you've been on Wall Street for even six months, you know you don't do this," Matthews said. "It's front running. Maybe it's not illegal, but it's definitely ethically dubious." BRKB off 1.5% at $84.18. (erik.holm@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:26 (Dow Jones) Canadian-based energy company \uc2 Pacific Rubiales\uc2  (PEGFF) has acquired a 50% interest in the Colombian assets of French company \uc2 Maurel & Prom\uc2 . Rubiales would be oh-so-pleased if investors view the move as a sign the company's focus has returned to exploring and drilling for oil following a dreadful month on the PR front that saw its share price plunge 25%. Rubiales' problems started with unexpectedly low 2010 production and reserves data and got worse when it surprised investors by "announcing" a years-old, but hardly-known, clause to its key Quifa contract that could pinch future earnings. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:21 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says resignation of David Sokol from \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  (BRKA BRKB) "again highlights the key-man risk involved in investing in BRK shares." \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 , the firm warns, "may be the only one who can manage the company with as much success as in the past." Sokol had been presumed to be the most likely candidate to replace Buffett as CEO. His departure moves other managers of BRKB subsidiaries higher on the list of candidates, including \uc2 Burlington Northern\uc2  CEO \uc2 Matt Rose\uc2  and MidAmerican CEO Greg Abel, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. BRKB off 1.5% at $84.18. (erik.holm@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:08 (Dow Jones) Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is losing the credibility battle on inflation, according to banking consultant Paulo Faria-Tavares of PTX Lending. "Interest rate futures are rising, which indicates inflation will keep accelerating," Faria-Tavares says. In recent statements, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has downplayed long-term inflation worries. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being tested by the market and it is losing credibility," he said. "They are forgetting the most important thing. They have to convince the market, ordinary people and economic agents, and they are failing." (tom.murphy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 (Dow Jones) Bombardier (BBD.B.T) up 11% after FY4Q results blow past consensus. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says company's results were strong, particularly its margins and cash. Aerospace is seeing clear signs of resurgent business jet demand, while transportation looks poised for growth on continued order strength, firm says. Scotia Capital agrees, noting aerospace was the major reason for the beat, with better-than-expected margins. In Toronto, BBD class B shares up 69 Canadian cents to C$6.99. (caroline.vanhasselt@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:02 (Dow Jones) Bullish USDA corn report is unable to lift Mosaic (MOS) shares after mixed F3Q earnings report. While prices soared for the fertilizer producer, volumes were weaker than expected. Earnings conference call at 10:00am EDT. USDA report today showed lower-than-expected supplies as of March 1, which has corn futures poised for a sharp rally. That normally boosts fertilizer, but MOS flat at $80.44. Rival CF Industries, meanwhile, up 4% to $137.88. (ian.berry@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrsl