\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Currency \highlight\uc2  Market Stronger On Low Volume,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeff Fick   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 21:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market trended stronger Monday, marked by a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had little effect on trading because of unusually low volume.\par \par \uc2 "Volume was super weak today, well below normal," said a Sao Paulo-based trader who asked not to be named. "There was a very small variation in the market, which is especially unusual for a week when futures contracts will expire."\par \par \uc2 Trading sessions at the end of each month are typically marked by high volume and increased volatility as long- and short-position holders battle to influence the Ptax, or the average daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate used to settle futures contracts. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also usually sits on the sidelines to not unfairly influence trading during the key month-end tug-of-war.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was much more than a wallflower during Monday's session, engaging in no less than four separate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank kicked off the day with a morning auction to buy dollars from the spot market, then in quick succession held an auction to buy forward-dollar contracts and reverse swaps. Late in the session, the bank held a second spot-market auction. The forward-dollar contract and reverse-swap auction were designed to roll over previous contracts coming due April 1, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "The volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  was heavy," said Jankiel Santos, an economist at investment bank BES Investimentos. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must have identified some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement expected in coming days."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows have been heavy in the first three months of 2011, topping the net dollar inflows from all of 2010. According to the latest data available, net dollar inflows through March 18 totaled $34.66 billion, outpacing the $24.35 billion that flowed into Brazil for all of last year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have tried to head off inflows related to recent overseas bond issues, which include a $500 million debt sale last week by a unit of closely held industrial conglomerate Odebrecht.\par \par \uc2 Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy hand, the real still was slightly stronger than Friday's close at BRL1.6605 to the dollar. The real traded at BRL1.6599 to the dollar as of 1825 GMT, according to Tullet Prebon via Factset. Many traders expect the real to remain rangebound between BRL1.65 and BRL1.70 to the dollar, the unofficial target range that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears willing to accept before issuing the market a stern response.\par \par \uc2 "The market has no real motive to push the dollar stronger," said the Sao Paulo-based trader. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows continue strong. And the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are practically daily, so the market has already priced them in."\par \par \uc2 While trading volume was light Monday, it should pick up Tuesday and Wednesday as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players take part in the Ptax battle and prepare to roll over positions, the trader said. That could add a measure of volatility ahead of the week's key indicator: the release of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly inflation report on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation report will shed some light on how the bank plans to fight growing inflationary pressures in Latin America's largest economy, economists at the local unit of Spain's Banco Santander said. Bank President Alexandre Tombini took a more hawkish tone last week, but continued to indicate the bank will look toward measures other than interest-rate increases to contain prices.\par \par \uc2 Banco Santander called the report a key piece of information, predicting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "might change its main inflation and growth forecasts in line with market expectations." That could give the bank, currently seen as behind the curve in the fight against inflation, more credibility with the market.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085, \par    Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 28-03-11 1945GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110328e73s0019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
