\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Currency \highlight\uc2  Market Stronger As  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Fade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeff Fick  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market traded stronger Monday as concerns about more government measures to contain the real's appreciation faded and dollar inflows remained strong.\par \par \uc2 While events in Japan, the Middle East and North Africa continued to attract attention from investors, the focus has shifted somewhat to the recurrent theme of strong foreign investment inflows into Brazil's resilient economy. Not even the first signs that U.S. interest rates could be heading higher were enough to undercut the real at the start of Monday's session.\par \par \uc2 Charles Plosser, president of the U.S. Federal Reserve Bank in Philadelphia, said Friday that interest rates in the world's largest economy would need to rise "in the not-too-distant future." Those comments were echoed by colleague James Bullard of the St. Louis Federal Reserve Bank, who suggested that it would be a "good idea" to discuss the end of the Fed's asset purchase program, dubbed quantitative easing II, at the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's April meeting.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's $600 billion plan to purchase U.S. Treasurys has created excess liquidity in the market that has undercut the value of the U.S. dollar, causing emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the world to appreciate in response. Higher U.S. interest rates, however, typically cause emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the real to weaken as investors opt for the safety of Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the market now expects the government to hold off implementing additional measures to contain the real to assess the impact of recent shocks to the global economy. The nuclear crisis in Japan in the wake of the massive 8.9 magnitude earthquake and tsunami continued Monday, with climbing radiation levels at a stricken reactor reigniting worries about how quickly the world's third-largest economy can recover from the disaster. Libyan rebels, meanwhile, continue to make gains against embattled dictator Moammar Gadhafi in the wake of the NATO-imposed no-fly zone over the country.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real traded stronger shortly after the open Monday at BRL1.6590 to the dollar, according to Tullet Prebon via Factset. That was stronger from Friday's close at BRL1.6605 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real remains rangebound between BRL1.65 and BRL1.70 to the dollar as the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy dollars on the spot market and banks trim short-dollar positions to meet reserve requirements implemented earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 This week, the real will likely trade between BRL1.65 to BRL1.68 to the dollar this week, "corresponding to the external scenario and depending on whether the global appetite for risk is more or less favorable," said Miriam Tavares, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  director at Sao Paulo-based AGK Corretora.\par \par \uc2 The market will gain further direction Wednesday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases its next quarterly inflation report. The report should give investors a better sense of the direction of future interest rates. Although recent comments by bank President Alexandre Tombini were more hawkish on inflation, many traders expect the bank to pursue alternative measures to fight inflation rather than relying solely on traditional interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 28-03-11 1230GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110328e73s0010c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
