\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  firms; Peru sol hit by political risk\b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 18:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen poised for further gains\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso firms 0.16 pct, Brazil real 0.06 pct\par \par \uc2 * Peru sol slides to 2-month low on election jitters\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Silvio Cascione\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, March 25 (Reuters) - Peru's sol hit a two-month low on Friday on worries about a resurgent leftist presidential hopeful, while Latin America's key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed poised to extend this week's rally.\par \par \uc2 Investors were concerned that major polls due on Sunday may confirm the second-place position of Peru's nationalist presidential candidate Ollanta Humala ahead of an April 10 vote.\par \par \uc2 "People are worried (Humala) could go after mining interests," said Dirk Willer, head of Latin America local markets strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Humala's surge to second place in a poll last Sunday spurred a sharp drop in the sol and a spike in the cost of insuring the country's bonds.\par \par \uc2 The sol <PEN=PEN> bid 0.18 percent weaker at 2.7880 and hit its weakest level against the dollar since January.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mexico's peso <MXN=> firmed 0.16 percent to 11.9355 after clearing the psychological level of 11.95 per dollar in the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Traders have set their sights on testing the peso's nearly 2-1/2-year high hit earlier this month at 11.8585 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso was on track to post a near 0.85 percent gain for the week, outpacing the rebound by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real -- where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears are weighing -- and Chile's peso.\par \par \uc2 Worries appeared to be fading that global growth could suffer a big setback from the ongoing unrest in the oil-producing Middle East and North African region, fears that Portugal will need a bailout and concerns about Japan's nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 Expectations are that monthly jobs data in the United States due next week could reinforce perceptions of a recovery there, which may back further gains for the region.\par \par \uc2 But if the data is much better than expected, some fear it will raise bets the United States may soon increase interest rates. Weak jobs data might hit expectations about U.S. demand for the region's exports.\par \par \uc2 "We should have a positive bias for the early part of next week and then if non-farm payrolls are not too strong and not too weak and along the lines of consensus, then the rally can continue," Willer said.\par \par \uc2 Latin America's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are broadly supported by high prices for the region's commodities, such as Chile's copper, Brazil's iron and soy and Mexico's oil. High interest rates as policymakers target inflation worries are also tempting yield-hungry global investors.\par \par \uc2 Mexico, which some market players see hiking interest rates as soon as this summer, is backed by the recovery of its manufacturing exports to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRBY> bid 0.06 percent stronger at 1.655 per dollar as it traded around a three-week high.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is inching back toward a 2-1/2-year high hit earlier this month that stirred concerns of fresh moves by authorities to tame the real's strength, which is hurting local manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 "The market is back to a level where it begins to have memories of a possible measure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Jose Carlos Amado, a trader at the Renaissance brokerage.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> bid 0.08 percent firmer at 479.30 per dollar, supported by a recovery in prices for copper, Chile's biggest export.\par \par \uc2 The peso hit a two-week high, but the cost of pesos in dollars bounced off a key support level at 478 per dollar that locals say is attracting firm bids for greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110328e73p0019f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
