\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso, Brazil real slump to 3-wk lows\b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Chile hits 7-week low on Japan nuclear crisis\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  cuts exposure to emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 10 pct\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  government delays \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso sheds 1.4 pct, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real 0.42 pct\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/NEW YORK, March 16 (Reuters) - Brazil and Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit three-week lows on Wednesday while Chile's peso fell to its weakest since January as concerns about Japan's nuclear crisis drove investors into safer-haven assets.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso, the most-liquid and easily settled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, slumped the most in one day since last May.\par \par \uc2 Adding pressure to Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was a \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  report recommending investors to cut exposure to emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 10 percent. [ID:nN16187257]\par \par \uc2 Selling of Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  accelerated as global officials sounded increasingly concerned over the situation\par \par \uc2 at the quake-stricken nuclear plant in Fukushima. [ID:nL3E7EF450] "This is panic and everything will depend on the developments out of Japan," said Alonso Madero, a fund manager at financial group Actinver in Mexico City. "Markets are overreacting and I think this will be transitory," he added.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso <MXN=> shed 1.84 percent to 12.21 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Fears that the global economic recovery may be choked off have been increasing due to Japan's disaster and growing violence in oil-producing countries.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Mexico's peso notched a nearly 2-1/2 year high, backed by an improving outlook for the U.S. economy. But some banks, like \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , have begun to revise down their U.S. growth forecasts. If the U.S. outlook worsens, the peso could lose support.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign investors were getting out of Mexico today," Rebeca Lizarraga, an analyst at Scotia Capital. "Risk aversion is getting really strong, because we were not seeing this before."\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso <CLP=CL> fell 0.51 percent to 486 per dollar. The peso has shed 3.7 percent from a high in late February amid concerns that unrest in the Middle East and North Africa will push oil prices higher. Chile imports almost all of its oil, unlike exporter Mexico and self-sufficient Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that Chilean policymakers will keep raising interest rates could help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  snap back if global jitters calm down.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise its benchmark rate by 25 basis points to 3.75 percent on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday its short-term inflation outlook has deteriorated because of global turmoil in the past month, which may back some market player bets on a 50 basis point hike this week.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY><BRL=> bid 0.42 percent weaker at 1.672 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Losses in the Brazilian real were cushioned, however, after a Finance Ministry source said the government would delay measures to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent appreciation trend while Japan's crisis unfolds. For details, see [ID:nN15275186].\par \par \uc2 A Finance Ministry source told Reuters late on Tuesday that the government discarded for now the announcement of new measures to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation because of global economic uncertainties stemming from Japan's crisis.\par \par \uc2 The new measures were expected to be unveiled as early as this week, after the real spiked to 2-1/2-year highs earlier this month. The real has shed 1.7 percent from that level, amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the crisis in Japan.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jose de Castro in Sao Paulo; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (REPEAT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110319e73g000t3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
