\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  sink on menace of Saudi unrest\b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 18:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso bounces off key 12/dlr level\par \par \uc2 * Investors eye possible new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real sheds 0.5 pct, Mexico peso 0.4 pct\par \par \uc2 * Chile peso slumps to weakest since early February\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 10 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened sharply on Thursday as reports of unrest in Saudi Arabia and sinking stock markets raised risks that the region's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be hit by rising global uncertainty.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real, already under pressure from concerns of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sank in international trade, while Mexico's peso managed to bounce off a key level, which may bode for a quick recovery if global tensions ease.\par \par \uc2 Reports of protest in Saudi Arabia, the top OPEC producer, revived concerns of further disruptions in oil supplies.\par \par \uc2 A sustained push higher in crude prices could weigh on global growth and push investors to dump riskier assets like emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and retreat into safe-haven assets like U.S. Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 "The situation in the Middle East is far from being resolved and it will be the main driver. The question is if there will be a sustained pickup in risk aversion or not," said David Beker, head of Latin American debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Bank of America-Merrill Lynch.\par \par \uc2 Fears drove the Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> down nearly 2 percent in its worst slump in seven months. [ID:nN10178483]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real <BRL=> shed 0.51 percent to 1.6618 per dollar in international trade. Losses seemed more contained on the local spot market <BRBY>, with the real off 0.24 percent at 1.659 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Investors remained leery of fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to keep the real's gains in check. Last week, the real closed at a 2-1/2 year high, stronger than what many see as the Finance Ministry's unofficial limit around 1.65 per dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadily escalated its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2009. But it has only slowed the ascent of the real, which has been buoyed by a tide of global investment that is drawn to its double-digit interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The real has firmed more than 40 percent since 2009.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> sank 0.8 percent to 481.50 per dollar, its weakest level since early February.\par \par \uc2 Chile's local spot market closed before news of the protest in Saudi Arabia, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hammered by falling prices for copper, Chile's main export, after a drop in Chinese import data cast doubt on demand from the world's top consumer of the metal.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it would allow more foreigners to issue bonds in pesos in the domestic market. [ID:nN10144530]\par \par \uc2 A trader said the policy would not likely have a major impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since local pension funds already buy foreign debt abroad.\par \par \uc2 Risk appetite had also been hurt during the session by renewed fears about Europe's debt after a rating agency downgrade of Spain.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> lost 0.38 percent to 11.9760 per dollar, paring steeper losses after bouncing off the key 12 per dollar level.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Mexico's peso hit its strongest since October 2008, breaking past the 11.95 level that had marked a range since January. With that support for the dollar knocked out, traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could set out a new range between 11.80 and 12 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 As long as oil prices do not spike further and stay high, hitting U.S. demand, the peso should benefit from the economic recovery in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the peso has lagged gains in other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and policymakers have not threatened to fight the further gains, unlike Brazil and Chile.\par \par \uc2 "The peso will still trend stronger. You do not find any other country in the region where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has given a green light to further \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," Beker said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jose de Castro in Sao Paulo; Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110313e73a000ls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
