42BC84C70C9887B236AF1643CDC717CBD3B2C814349CB139F3B86C3172CB21F0A67544176DF4D14827AD34751100003B}}
\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Markets Face Possible  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Murphy   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 15:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Brazilian financial markets will wake up from the annual Carnival holiday on Wednesday afternoon with a hangover, composed of one-part European fiscal worry and two parts domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Trading in stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and futures and options contracts will resume at 1600 GMT for the first time since the Friday close.\par \par \uc2 "There will be plenty of work for everyone," said Paulo Faria-Tavares, managing director of Sao Paulo's PTX Lending consultants. "A lot has happened this week -- Greece heavily downgraded, oil about to reach $120 a barrel, Libya fighting and new Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, possibly Wednesday or Thursday."\par \par \uc2 The latter possibility is probably the most worrisome for investors, at least in the short term.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real closed Friday at BRL1.6424 to the dollar, slightly stronger against the Thursday close and breaking the key BRL1.65 barrier for the first time in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Over the last two years, the real has gained more than 35% against the dollar, leading to worries about declining exports and increasing competition from cheap imports of manufactured products.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market a number of times in recent months to contain the appreciation of the real, most notably in October when it raised taxes on short-term inflows destined for the fixed-income market.\par \par \uc2 "We would not at all be surprised to see new measures unveiled this week," according to \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  strategist Win Thin, in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Local news reports indicate new measures could include higher taxes on a broader range of inflows, aggressive new government purchases of dollars from the market or even a quarantine on some forms of foreign investment inflows.\par \par \uc2 "Implications for the BRL [Brazilian real] are negative on expectations of outflows and regulatory risk similar to that following the revisions in the IOF [financial transactions tax] in October," said analysts at RBS Securities in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Thin agreed that, in the short term, the trend for the Brazilian real might be negative, even including a rollback to BRL1.70 to the dollar. But he noted that the medium-term trend is still toward appreciation of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Selic base interest rate is a towering 11.75%. Rates at this level have proven highly attractive to foreign investors at a time when U.S. and European interest rates are near zero.\par \par \uc2 In a research note, economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  noted Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will have to continue to push rates higher in order to get a grip on deteriorating inflation dynamics."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's inflation rate is currently 6.0%, well above the government's 2011 target of 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazilian stocks will have to contend with increasing worries about the European fiscal crisis. Moody's Investors Service earlier this week downgraded Greece's sovereign rating for the second time in a year, this time to B1 with a "negative outlook."\par \par \uc2 Fiscal worries also continue to dog Portugal.\par \par \uc2 Volatile international oil prices in the face of a virtual civil war in Libya will likely increase risk aversion among foreign investors, according to analysts.\par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 03-09-11 0918ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110309e7390003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
