\par\par \b \uc2 Heavy  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Action Holds Brazil Real To Small Gain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Murphy   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 21:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--As in past sessions, heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action Wednesday helped hold the Brazilian real to only a small gain against the U.S. dollar as investors weighed possible effects of an expected interest rate hike.\par \par \uc2 The real exited from active trading at BRL1.6585 to the dollar, representing a small gain against Tuesday's close of BRL1.6620, according to SIX \uc2 Telekurs\uc2  via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday night, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to review its Selic base rate, currently a towering 11.25%. Most analysts expect a half-point hike to 11.75% as part of a government drive to pull down inflation. Brazil's inflation rate is currently 6.1%.\par \par \uc2 "It's inevitable that this [rate hike] will attract more investment inflows into Brazil," said Luiz Henrique Didier, partner of the Didier-Levy brokerage in Sao Paulo. "This is especially true given that interest rates in many traditional investment venues are near zero."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures released Wednesday underscored continued investor interest. The figures showed net inflows of U.S. dollars for the period Feb. 1 through Feb. 25 of $6.3 billion. During the same period a year ago, Brazil faced net outflows of $399 million.\par \par \uc2 But just as Brazilian government officials have been adamant about controlling inflation, they have also stated explicitly their distaste for any further appreciation of the Brazilian real.\par \par \uc2 "This is a delicate problem," said Demosthenes Madureira Pinho, director of asset management at Itau, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's biggest private bank. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, affecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to be just right. Too much \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation hurts Brazilian exports; too much depreciation sparks inflation."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday was entirely in the direction of attempting to prevent further appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars from the spot market in two separate auctions. It also bought forward delivery contracts for dollars at an additional pair of auctions.\par \par \uc2 In addition to an expected interest rate hike, the real was also favored Wednesday by continued weakening of the U.S. dollar in world markets. The dollar lost out to the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on continued Middle East tensions, increasing global oil prices and rising risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 Trading on Brazilian credit markets Wednesday reflected market expectations for a half-point \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate hike. On the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, interest rate futures contracts closed mostly higher. The contracts reflect investor expectations for annualized interest rates at future dates. Among actively traded interest rate futures contracts, January 2012 closed at 12.53%, up from 12.50% Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 02-03-11 2026GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110302e7320019d\par }