\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2011\par 02:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBNZ: WORKING HARD TO ASSIST RECOVERY, ENSURE MARKETS REMAIN STABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reserve Bank of New Zealand said Wednesday it is working hard to assist the recovery and ensure markets remain stable, after the 6.3 magnitude earthquake that struck New Zealand's second largest city just after midday Tuesday. (published 2349 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN POSTS 1ST TRADE DEFICIT IN 22 MONTHS ON WEAK EXPORTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a rare trade deficit in January, as weaker-than-expected exports and a sharp increase in import costs pushed the numbers into the red for the first time in 22 months. (published 0055 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER: GLOBAL ECONOMIC CONDITIONS TURNING WORSE FOR DOMESTIC INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Yoon Jeung-hyun said Wednesday the recent spike in prices of global oil and raw materials is negatively affecting the local economy by adding to increasing inflationary pressure. (published 0033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBA'S STEVENS ARGUES FOR SAVINGS TO DEAL WITH INCOME SURGE INTO ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glenn Stevens\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday urged that savings growth continue as a means of allowing the domestic economy to deal with the mining investment boom, adding it is impossible to say how long the boom will last and that the economic expansions of China and India are unlikely to be short-lived. (published 2200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA 4Q WAGE INDEX +1.0% ON QTR VS CONSENSUS +0.9% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian wages excluding bonuses rose a seasonally adjusted 1.0% in the fourth quarter of 2010 from the third quarter and rose 3.9% from a year earlier, Australian Bureau of Statistics labor price data issued Wednesday show. (published 0031 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN BUILDING ACTIVITY FALLS 5.1% IN 4Q VS 3Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of building work completed in Australia fell 5.1% to A$20.50 billion in the fourth quarter of 2010 from the third quarter, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Wednesday. (published 0033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA & NZ BANKING GROUP PRICES DOLLAR BONDS TO YIELD 3.339% -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia & NZ Banking Group Ltd. priced the sale of its five-year global bond offering on Tuesday to yield 3.339%, according to a person familiar with the deal. (published 2246 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: US CONSUMER CONFIDENCE INDEX HITS 3-YEAR HIGH IN FEB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. consumers felt better about the economy this month, according to a report released Tuesday. But rising energy prices could limit future gains. The Conference Board, a private research group, said its index of consumer confidence jumped to 70.4 in February from a revised 64.8 in January. (published 1543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: SENATE PANEL TO KICK OFF TAX CODE REVIEW NEXT WEEK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Senate Finance Committee next week will embark on what is likely to be an exhaustive review of the U.S. tax code, as it hears from a quartet of former Treasury tax officials. (published 0030 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: ECB PROVOPOULOS: AGAINST GREEK DEBT RESTRUCTURING, WANTS BANK MERGERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 George Provopoulos, a member of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Governing Council, said Tuesday he remains opposed to a Greek debt restructuring but would like 