\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Flexes  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Muscle In  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeff Fick   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2011\par 21:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market appeared ready to shrug off ongoing violence in North Africa and the Middle East on Tuesday, but a surprise reverse-swap auction by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a message of defiance from Libya was enough to undercut foreign-investment inflows.\par \par \uc2 The latest hostilities in an ongoing wave of pro-democracy protests shocked the international community, as reports of Libyan Air Force pilots bombing their countrymen and indiscriminate shooting of protesters sent cautious investors scurrying for safety. Concerns about supply interruptions sent international oil prices skyrocketing, with crude oil for April delivery jumping 4.5%, to $96.45 a barrel on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But fears of a broader international impact were subdued in Brazil, where local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders confirmed a correction in early weakness for the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The real strengthened throughout the session on expectations for increased foreign-investment inflows, including net inflows of about $460 million into Brazil's main stock exchange through Feb. 18.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, reminded the market of its might via a surprise reverse-swap auction, traders said. The bank sold the equivalent of $848.2 million in reverse swap contracts on offer at three different maturities. It was the eighth reverse swap auction held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year. At a reverse swap auction on Feb. 18, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 86% of the contracts offered, worth $1.48 billion.\par \par \uc2 "The bank typically announces reverse-swap auctions when the market is closed," said Alberto Orsovay, a dealer at Sao Paulo-based brokerage Hencorp Commcor. "You could say that the market was expecting a reverse-swap auction, but not during trading hours."\par \par \uc2 Reverse swap auctions offer investors the opportunity to exchange futures positions in U.S. dollars for positions tied to domestic interest rates. Typically, a reverse swap auction supports the dollar against the Brazilian real by removing future dollar deliveries from the market. The government revived reverse swap auctions earlier this year in an effort to arrest the continued appreciation of the Brazilian real against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then followed up the reverse-swap auction with a snap auction to buy dollars from the spot market. The bank purchased an undisclosed volume of dollars from the spot market at BRL1.6706 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Also undermining the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at about the same time was embattled Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi's pledge to go down fighting. In a speech broadcast on Libyan state TV, Gadhafi vowed to keep his hold on power and defied protesters to end his rule. The country also shut its ports.\par \par \uc2 Libya's descent into violence has had a greater impact than conflicts elsewhere because of the country's position as a member of the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries. Libya produces about 1.8 million barrels of crude a day and has 41 billion barrels of proven reserves, about 3% of global supply.\par \par \uc2 The double-dip \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Gadhafi's speech combined to undercut the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traded weaker at BRL1.6695 to the dollar as of 1848 GMT, according to \uc2 Telekurs\uc2  via Factset. The real closed Monday at BRL1.6675 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real likely would have ended even weaker if not for support from the latest inflation figures, which showed that consumer prices continued to grow through mid-February. The mid-month inflation rate was 0.97% through mid-February, versus a 0.76% advance through mid-January. The rolling 12 month inflation rate climbed to 6.08%, well above the government's official target of 4.5%. That will likely mean higher near-term interest rates in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "The market is pricing in higher interest rates," said Hencorp Commcor's Orsovay. "The trend is still for the real to strengthen on investment inflows."\par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085, Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 22-02-11 2012GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110222e72m001g4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
