\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Brazil Dollar Inflows Surge In Jan To $15.5B\b0\par\par\uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2011\par 22:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with analyst comments) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Gerald Jeffris \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Brazil continued to register voluminous dollar flows into its economy in January, revealing the limited effect of recent government efforts to curb strong incoming investment and a strengthened local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to data released Wednesday by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , net dollar receipts in the month rose to $15.51 billion from only $1.08 billion in the same month last year, reaching their highest monthly level since June 2007.\par \par \uc2 Analysts, meanwhile, noted the result was a good sign abundant global liquidity would help Brazil finance its growing current account deficit, even as it struggled to limit appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This strong inflow in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  spot markets attests that despite growing governmental \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to attract massive capital inflows," said \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Luis Cezario.\par \par \uc2 Dollar inflows began the month strong and surged still more late in the period as investors appeared to call the bluff on the government's recent moves to discourage heavy portfolio investment inflows amid prospects of rising domestic interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported incoming investment in the month totaled $14.44 billion, while net inflows from foreign trade totaled $1.08 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the same period last year, Brazil saw investment dollar inflows of $1.22 billion and net trade outflows of $140 million.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government so far this year has gone to extra efforts to discourage an influx of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the economy, introducing forward dollar purchase auctions to help delay entrance of dollars and "reverse" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that allow investors to exchange dollar-indexed bonds for paper linked to domestic interest rates.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of some market reaction, however, the trend for strong incoming dollar flows appeared to slow slightly in the first four days of February. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported dollar inflows in that period totaled only $39 million, compared to $1.86 billion in the same period last year. Early February result was based on net disinvestment of $118 million and incoming trade flows of $157 million.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sach's Cezario said given the heavy dollar inflows this year to date, the government could be forced to introduce still more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  control measures to avoid further strengthening of the real.\par \par \uc2 "In all, we believe that the government will allow the Brazilian real to remain around BRL1.65 per U.S. dollar for a while, perhaps tolerating a slow appreciation toward BRL1.60 while inflation continues to be a concern," he said. "However, as the real continues to appreciate, we believe that the government may implement additional measures to curb capital inflows and arrest its appreciation, such as increases in IOF taxation on foreign investment and the introduction of more capital and reserve requirements on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions."\par \par \uc2 With the heavy incoming dollar flows in January, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday reported it purchased $7.99 billion in spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions to build its foreign reserves. In the first four days of February, the bank purchased $2.84 billion.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported local banks reduced their short dollar positions to $11.02 billion in January from $16.78 billion the previous month. The reduction came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased bank's reserve requirements on short positions.\par \par \uc2 Net foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flowing into Brazil ended 2010 at $24.35 billion in 2010, down from $28.73 billion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Still, heavy incoming portfolio investment inflows have continued to limit the government's success in protecting local industries from accelerating imports and a difficult outlook for exports brought by a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, has gained more than 30% against the dollar over the past 24 months, ending Wednesday's trading at BRL1.6599 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (5561) 3335-0832, gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 09, 2011 16:58 ET (21:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110209e729006yp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
