\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Finance Minister: Budget Cuts Still Under Study\b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 07:52 EDT *DJ  Brazil Finance Minister To Announce Budget Cuts Early Feb\par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 07:54 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fin Min: \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  A Necessity\par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 08:18 EDT =DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister: Budget Cuts Still Under Study\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's government is still studying 2011 budget cuts, with no specific target figure in mind, and will announce the size and shape of the cuts in early February, Finance Minister Guido Mantega told reporters Friday.\par \par \uc2 "There is no specific figure," he said, in a tape-recorded interview circulated by the ministry. "I can't tell you that it's 30 billion or 40 billion or 50 billion...We will announce the cuts in early February."\par \par \uc2 Press reports earlier Friday indicated the government was focused on cuts totaling some 50 billion Brazilian reais ($30 billion).\par \par \uc2 "We will aim for the maximum possible savings in the area of government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "Social programs, health and education will be preserved."\par \par \uc2 Many economists have called for budget cuts as a way to counter rising Brazilian inflation. Inflation ended 2010 at 5.9%, well above the government's target for the year of 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 Mantega also commented on rising concerns over the appreciation of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar. The real has gained more than 30% against the dollar since 2009. The strong real hurts Brazilian exports.\par \par \uc2 "This is the time for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market," Mantega said, defending the government's decision Thursday to hold so-called reverse swap auctions. "The reverse swap auction is a classic device for reducing pressure on the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold a reverse swap auction later Friday. The auction permits investors to exchange dollar positions in the futures market for positions tied to interest rates. The reduction in dollar positions reduces speculation and volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7090; brazil@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 14, 2011 08:18 ET (13:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110114e71e002he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
