\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Fin Min: High Commodity Prices Driving Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 16:11 EDT *DJ  Brazil's Mantega: Recent Floods Will Have Impact On Food Prices\par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 16:12 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Mantega: \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-Exchange Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Was Necessary\par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 16:12 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Mantega: Dollar Would Be At BRL1.45-1.50 Without Action\par \par \uc2 14 Jan 2011 16:36 EDT =DJ  Brazil Finance Minister: High Commodity Prices Driving Inflation\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)-Rising commodity prices were the driving force behind inflationary pressures in Brazil, which will also be affected by recent floods, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said late Friday.\par \par \uc2 "In 2010 and the start of 2011, we had inflation in food prices that hit practically all of the countries in Latin America," Mantega said. Inflation, however, wasn't based on supply--Brazil had its largest harvest ever in 2010--but on global demand for commodities, the minister said.\par \par \uc2 New President Dilma Rousseff and her cabinet have pledged to fight rising prices in Brazil, where consumer prices surged 5.9% in 2010--well above the government's official target of 4.5%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets Wednesday, when it is expected to raise interest rates to cool price pressures.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, the recent natural disaster in the mountains northwest of the beachfront city of Rio de Janeiro will likely also have an impact on food prices in the near term, Mantega said. More than 500 people were killed in the flooding and landslides.\par \par \uc2 "I imagine that, in the areas where there was flooding, they are going to have elevated [food] prices," the minister said.\par \par \uc2 The rise in global commodity prices was also hitting export countries, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are appreciating rapidly, according to Mantega. Brazil's government has recently implemented measures aimed at stopping speculative inflows and supporting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called the real.\par \par \uc2 "The measures are working," Mantega said. "We've been able to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its current level."\par \par \uc2 The real, which closed trading Friday at BRL1.6825 to the dollar, would likely be trading between BRL1.45 and BRL1.50 to the dollar without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government, Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 Mantega was part of a cabinet meeting with Rousseff earlier Thursday, where he presented the Finance Ministry's five-year economic outlook for Brazil.\par \par \uc2 According to Mantega, the new president didn't discuss a raise in the minimum salary, which would stay at BRL545.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 14, 2011 16:36 ET (21:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110114e71e0052d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
