\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: BRL Weakens On  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 13:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1233 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil's real weakens as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will Friday step up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, by means of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reverse swap auction. This will be the first time in more than 1 1/2 years that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will enter the derivatives market. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will buy up to $1 billion worth of futures contracts. "All \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  futures market impacts the spot market given the fact that the futures market has much more liquidity," says Mario Battistel, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Fair Corretora de Cambio. BRL/USD trades 0.59119 from the day's high of 0.59790. (Rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1225 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads drift listlesly remaining broadly flat on Friday at 815 bps, as the market absorbs recent sharp 170 bps fall over the week from historic highs. Turnover is muted at only EUR7M on the local HDAT and European trading platform. "After the recent rally the market is pausing for breath to determine if the bullish trend has further to go," says a local bond analyst. "Market direction next week will be determined by news-flow from Europe, with all eyes on any subtle comments emerging from the Euro-group meeting," he adds. (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1220 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD has added a few more ticks in the wake of \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 's (JPM) impressive 4Q results, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says, and one may be linked with the other. EUR/USD slips to the session's low of 1.3334, GBP/USD drops some 10 ticks to 1.5832, and USD/CHF trades at the day's high of 0.9674. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1206 GMT [Dow Jones] Today's Swiss government meeting with representatives from the business community and unions is unlikely to produce major measures but "may have a psychological effect on trade," a Zurich trader says. "I think this is more of a political stunt that should show that the government is aware of the Swiss franc strength," he adds. Says that he doubts that SNB will bow to political pressure. EUR/CHF currently at 1.2919 and USD/CHF at 0.9662. (goran.mijuk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1156 GMT [Dow Jones] Chile's peso was trading weaker versus the dollar early Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its benchmark interest rate steady at 3.25%. Traders believe the bank kept the rate unchanged to help support its recently announced $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program for 2011. "The bank is backing up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program with this move. It doesn't say so explicitly, but it's understood," says Matias Madrid, chief economist with local investment bank Banco Penta. In recent trading the peso was at CLP492.70, versus Thursday's close of CLP489.50, according to local newswire Valor Futuro. (anthony.esposito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1142 GMT [Dow Jones] Yes, Mr Trichet, talk the anti-inflation talk. But, no Mr Trichet, don't walk the anti-inflation walk, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. First, there isn't any evidence that the rally in global commodity prices, the main driver of inflation pressure at the moment, will persist. Second, higher interest rates might be good for Germany or even for France but they are hardly what the rest of the euro zone needs. And third, that 5 cent rise in the EUR this week might already be doing a bit of the monetary-tightening trick for you. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1135 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  expects China to continue with its incremental and multi-front approach in managing excess liquidity through tight controls on capital inflows, RRR hikes and sterilization, and tightened credit supply, especially to the real estate sector. The investment house predicts two more RRR hikes, three more interest rate hikes in 2011 (with the next one in 1Q), as well as further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation (with year-end USD/CNY forecast at 6.300), as policy response to inflation pressure and rebounding domestic demand. It believes one possible policy response to the inflation pressure could be the front-loading of yuan appreciation to curb imported inflation pressure. (victoria.ruan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1132 GMT [Dow Jones] The lack of bearish traction in U.S. interest rates gives warning that USD/JPY will trade back to 82.00 or even lower, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . However, the bank says it would look to buy any dip against support in the 81.10-81.00 area for an eventually rally toward 84.00-84.50. USD/JPY now at 82.78. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1127 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD took a hit after the China RRR hike however Citi says there is chatter in the market of Sovereign demand and this has helped lift the spot to 0.9910. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1120 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro Overnight Index Average fixed a tad lower Thursday at 0.375% versus Wednesday's 0.376%. Friday, the interbank overnight rate is trading at 0.30%-0.35%, at the upper end of Thursday's 0.25%-0.35% range. The use of ECB's deposit facility hit the highest level in the current reserve period Thursday, signalling excess liquidity in the market. After the end of the reserve period Tuesday, traders expect liquidity to get tighter, leading to a rise in rates. Earlier Friday, 3-month Euribor fixed at 1.006% vs 0.998% Thursday. (neetha.mahadevan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1118 GMT [Dow Jones] Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step in with more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases as the ruble nears 34.50 versus a basket of dollars and euros, Renaissance Capital says. The ruble will also receive support from Russia's trade surplus and decreased capital outflows, the investment bank says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying between $150 million and $200 million daily to smooth out exchange rate volatility, traders say. Ruble at RUB35.56 against the basket, weaker by 0.3%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1113 GMT [Dow Jones] China's shares are likely to open slightly lower Monday in a knee-jerk reaction to the latest hike in banks' reserve requirement ratio. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index may test its immediate psychological support at 2750, after closing down 1.3% at 2791.34 today. "The RRR hike is kind of within expectations because of the strong growth in new yuan loan issuance last month. But compared with an interest rate hike, the move will have a smaller impact on stocks," says Amy Lin, an analyst at Capital Securities. (amy.li@dowjones.com) [ 01-14-11 0733ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110114e71e0008c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
