\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Could Cause More Volatility In EM - RBC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,116 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2011\par 16:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1538 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging-market debt slightly weaker versus Treasurys, showing little response to US jobs data. The risk premium on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global is three basis points wider to 275 basis points over Treasurys. The index is down 0.18% in price terms. RBC analysts note that EM asset performance has been mixed in the last couple weeks and that the primary concern holding investors back is uncertainty over how the euro-zone sovereign periphery crisis may unfold. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1537 GMT [Dow Jones] Nearest 1-yr calendar spread even tighter as Eurodollar futures traders view Bernanke's comments and disappointing jobs data as signs short-term rates won't be rising much anytime soon. Mar 2012 contract recently prices in rate 66.5 BP higher than rate priced into Mar '11 contract. Same spread at 69 BP just before release of Bernanke's remarks, and at 74 BP at Thurs' settlement. Price for quarterly 2011 Eurodollar contracts recently up 1.5 to 7.5 BP. 2012 contracts recently up as much as 12 BP. (Howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1535 GMT [Dow Jones] Munis have a slightly weaker tone, with 10-yr bonds appearing to see the most pressure, says \uc2 Miller Tabak\uc2 's Michael Pietronico. "There's a fair amount of selling for a Friday," he says. "Probably a lot has to do with positioning post payrolls." Still, trading is thin, and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Wealth Mgmt's Gary Pollack estimates 10-yr bonds just a bp or so weaker thus far. "I think there's a lot of fear in the muni market," Pollack adds. Mutual funds continue to lose money, he added, and next week's calendar is much healthier, with an estimated $3.4B in negotiated sales, according to Ipreo LLC.\par \par \uc2 (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1533 GMT [Dow Jones] RBC analysts note that within emerging markets there is clearly endorsement building among policymakers on more market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  or protectionist policies, as seen in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Chile and Israel this week. The analysts believe this trend will only deepen in the short term, which could inject higher volatility and downward pressures on EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and EM local financial assets, bonds and equities, in particular. (anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1530 GMT [Dow Jones] Growth in the ECRI weekly leading index strengthened further in latest week. The growth rate for the week of December 31 improved to 3.3% from 2.3%, but the index itself slipped to 128.9 from 129.0. Growth in the December index strengthened to 2.3% from -2.3% in November. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1525 GMT [Dow Jones] Bernanke and payrolls report galvanize Treasury steepening yield curve trade. Some investors buy short-dated notes and sell long-dated bonds as the latter bears the brunt of next week's new debt sales, traders say. With Benernake still signaling a slow recovery, interest rates futures cut bets on a hike in fed-funds target rate by end of 2011 and that gives the two-year Treasury a boost because the yield is the most sensitive to changes in Fed rate outlook.\par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays\uc2  topped the list of bookrunners for US asset-backed deals valued at $23.1 mln in 2010, according to Asset-Backed Alert, a trade publication. \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  took the next two slots. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  was also the top manager for ABS, with 79 deals valued at $62.29 mln. RBS and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  took the second and third slots.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1524 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds are slightly higher, having flipped between positive and negative territory as traders try and make sense of the nonfarm payrolls report. While the headline nonfarm payrolls number fell short of estimates (+103k vs +150k forecast), the unemployment rate declined to 9.4%, the lowest level since May 2009. Bunds also got support from widening yield spreads on peripheral euro zone government bonds and non-core borrowers may stay under pressure ahead of bond supply next week, with Portugal, Italy, and Spain all tapping the market. March bunds are up 0.12 at 125.85 and the 10-year bund is little changed at 96.54, yielding 2.904%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1520 GMT [Dow Jones] The ADP private-sector wmployment data's miss on Dec NFP today marks the "death knell" for the ADP report and the final tolerance for using it as a preliminary guide to nonfarm payroll expectations, says Scotia Capital. "This is the worst performance that ADP has put in by way of a preliminary first-pass guide to nonfarm," Scotia says. Despite ADP's guidance that private payroll jobs rose almost 300K in Dec, nonfarm private payrolls rose only 113K, Scotia says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1512 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY erases gains on day and declines slightly after limp jobs data. Blame that on QE2 expectations, says Steven Englander of Citi. "The markets now think it's likely that the Fed will go all the way on QE," says Englander. After this data, forget about the outside chance of ending the $600 billion asset-purchase program prematurely, he says. USD/JPY at 83.15 from 83.69 before data and 83.36 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1510 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell USD/JPY at 83.26 with a stop on a daily close above 84.34 and a take profit level at 80.23 says Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets. The bank says the reason is that the rates long-term decline is resuming after failing at major trend resistance and a short-term sell signal has just been completed. USD/JPY now at 83.13 from the day's high of 83.70. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1509 GMT [Dow Jones] Mexico's peso is slightly stronger against the dollar following the U.S. December payrolls disappointment, with USD/MXN recently at 12.2170, according to CQG, compared with 12.2199 Thursday when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was hit by a broad rally in the dollar. On the local front, the Bank of Mexico is set to report inflation for December. An expected 0.39% rise in the CPI would bring full-year 2010 inflation to 4.29%--within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forecast range for the fourth quarter. (anthony.harrup@dowjones.com) [ 01-07-11 1039ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110107e717000aq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
