\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Slips Early On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threat, Factors Abroad\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2011\par 13:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real weakened in early trading Wednesday as investors reacted cautiously to threats of local market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government and uncertainties abroad.\par \par \uc2 As of 1200 GMT, the real traded at BRL1.667 to the dollar after ending at BRL1.662 to the dollar Tuesday according to \uc2 Telekurs\uc2  via Factset.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the market was closely monitoring local government moves after Finance Minister Guido Mantega renewed threats Tuesday to take measures to curb the strength of the real.\par \par \uc2 "The government always has a group of measures prepared for the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  area," Mantega said. "If there's a greater deterioration, we'll take additional measures."\par \par \uc2 Mantega said recent exchange rate levels have been harmful to the country's exports and trade balance and pledged to maintain Brazil 2011 trade surplus above the $20 billion posted in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government in October raised the country's IOF Financial \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tax on foreign investment in fixed-income and certain derivatives in an effort to discourage incoming investment and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Alongside possible local actions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also influenced Wednesday by market declines abroad and falling commodities prices.\par \par \uc2 Also on the horizon for investors is the possibility of interest rate tightening in other emerging markets such as China. Rate hikes abroad, analysts note, could siphon off some of the heavy foreign invesment that has pushed Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recent highs.\par \par \uc2 Brazil itself, however, could see the beginning of its own cycle of rate hikes at a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy committee meeting later this month as 12-month local inflation continues to remain above the government's official 4.5% inflation target.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen maintaining a presence in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday with its daily dollar purchase auctions to build foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's reserves as of Tuesday stood at $289.1 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (5561) 3335-0832, gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 05-01-11 1235GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1836 5053 5076   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110105e715000up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
