\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Venezuela devalues; Chile's peso gains\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2010\par 00:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Chile's peso top gains for 2010 in Latam \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * Brazil's real jumps on month-end positioning\par \par \uc2  * Mexican, Colombian pesos also set to beat real in 2010  (Recasts, adds comment; dateline previously SANTIAGO/SAO PAOLO)\par \par \uc2  By Michael O'Boyle and Samantha Pearson\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Venezuela devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday in a move that will help government finances and could help support the country's volatile debt in 2011, while Chile's peso rose to a 31-month high.\par \par \uc2  Venezuela's benchmark 2027 global bond <VENGLB27=RR> has rallied more than a percentage point this month, helped by higher oil prices and advances in President Hugo Chavez's bid to raise taxes next year. Analysts said the devaluation help spur could further gains early next year.\par \par \uc2  "The tax increases and the devaluation will help the government position in the year ahead ... and should benefit Venezuelan assets, " said Alejandro Arreaza, an analyst at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York\par \par \uc2  The devaluation was the second such move in the last 12 months and it will push down local costs for the state run-oil company, improving its financial flexibility and the government's fiscal position.  For details, see [ID:nN30116504]\par \par \uc2  Venezuela may not issue any new dollar debt in the first half of 2011, and scarcity of the country's high-yielding debt should further underpin the market for its bonds, Arreaza said.\par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, Chile's peso highlighted its position as the top gainer among major Latin American peers, after the surging price of copper pushed it to a 31-month high on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  Chile is the world's top copper producer and has been flooded with dollars as investors bet commodity prices will keep rising in 2011.\par \par \uc2  High copper prices have kept local authorities from matching \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts seen in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Colombia to tame \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains that are hurting local exporters. [ID:nN30111514]\par \par \uc2  Demand for Latin American debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been fueled by low interest rates in major economies that are trying to spur growth and underpin a recovery from the 2008 financial crisis. Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains have raised concerns that asset bubbles could be forming in the region.\par \par \uc2  The Chilean peso <CLP=> firmed 0.24 percent to 467.70 per dollar on Thursday, closing the trading year 8.4 percent stronger.\par \par \uc2  "The peso could approach 465 in the first few days of 2011, but the market sees this as a critical level and after that it could start to reverse and test 470," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Santiago.\par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real <BRBY> surged 0.83 percent against the dollar in the local spot market on month-end positioning to trade at 1.664. Brazil, Latin America's biggest economy, continues to post strong economic growth and draw investors.\par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real is heading toward a more than two-year high despite efforts by authorities to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2  The real gained 4.6 percent in 2010, underperforming its peers due to dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as uncertainty about a presidential election and the world's biggest share offering by state-run oil firm \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Colombia's peso <COP=> gained 0.63 percent to 1,924.90 to the dollar on the last day of local trading, closing the year with a 5.8 percent gain.\par \par \uc2  The Colombian peso often outperformed its peers this year, but was battered down from a more than two-year high hit in October as local authorities took measures to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The peso plunged early this week as a scarcity of dollars was overwhelmed by demand for greenbacks on end-of-year corporate demand.\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <MXN=> edged up 0.04 percent to 12.3925 per dollar on Thursday, heading for a 5.7 percent gain for the year on the back of growth in the United States, which buys most of the exports from Latin America's second biggest economy.  (Additional reporting by Froilan Romero in Santiago; Editing by Leslie Adler)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)(REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110102e6cu000a5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
