\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Ends Stronger On Lift From Commodities, Market Trends\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2010\par 20:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real gained back some recently lost ground to end stronger against the dollar Monday as elevated commodities prices and trends in markets around the globe brought fresh inflows of funds locally.\par \par \uc2 The real ended at BRL1.697 to the dollar on the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange after ending at BRL1.7195 to the dollar Friday.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the real began the session strengthening as investors took encouragement from China's resolve to keep interest rates steady over the weekend after a tightening of bank reserve requirements late last week.\par \par \uc2 Expectations of a still-strong Chinese economy also helped give lift to prices of metals and other major commodities, of which Brazil is a key exporter.\par \par \uc2 The global and local rate outlook, meanwhile, continues to be an important focus for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Analysts note the U.S. Federal Reserve's open market committee isn't expected to unveil any surprises this week, with interest rates likely to remain low for an "extended period" and the bond-buying program likely to stay in place. That trend should help maintain investor appetite for Brazilian assets, which have provided an attractive alternative to investments elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Locally, analysts are mulling whether or when Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will start to raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's general price index, known as the IGP-M, rose 0.83% in the Nov. 21-30 period compared with an increase of 0.79% during the Oct. 21-31 period, the private Getulio Vargas Foundation said Monday. The latest figure was in line with analysts' expectations, and pushed the 12-month figure up to 11.48%.\par \par \uc2 The IGP-M index is used to adjust a number of contracts in Brazil, including rental agreements and utilities rates.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian financial market analysts and economists once again raised their 2010 and 2011 year-end average inflation forecasts, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil's market survey published Monday.\par \par \uc2 They see the IPCA consumer price inflation rate at 5.85% by year's end, up from 5.78% a week earlier and above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target of 4.5% for the year. In addition, economists increased their average estimate for 2011 inflation to 5.21% from 5.20%.\par \par \uc2 The survey, however, showed that the economists still expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key interest rate, the Selic, to end 2011 at 12.25%, suggesting 1.50 percentage points of rate hikes from the current 10.75%.\par \par \uc2 With a likelihood of rising rates locally, investors have remained eager to pump foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market.\par \par \uc2 The government, meanwhile, has managed to maintain the real at or near the BRL1.70 mark with recent threats of stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the IOF tax on foreign investment in fixed-income securities to 6% from 2% and also raised the tax for guarantees on derivatives investments. Late last week, Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega reiterated a pledge to step in against the real should it strengthen significantly.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, did its part Monday also to help curb the strengthening real with a customary dollar purchase auction to build foreign reserves. The bank bought an undisclosed amount of dollars in a late session auction at a rate of BRL1.7001.\par \par \uc2 As of Friday, Brazil's reserves stood at $285.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; 5561-3335-0832; gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 12-13-10 1443ET ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101213e6cd000hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
